WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.380
ALAIN VIEL: In the next set of videos, forming unit three of your course,

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:18.660
our goal is to discuss how cells harvest energy from fuel molecules,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.650
and also to show that, at the most fundamental level,

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:28.410
the exergonic transfer of electrons, through a series of protein complexes,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.950
powers the endergonic synthesis of ATP.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.730
In these videos, I would like to give you a quick overview of key pathways,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.800
leading to the synthesis of ATP.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.600
So don&amp;#39;t worry about the specifics for now.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.180
We have plenty of time for that later.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.380
Based on my introduction, two concepts emerge.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:02.890
First, molecules have to give electrons, and second, some molecules

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:06.140
have to receive these electrons.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.630
Let&amp;#39;s identify what are the electron donating and accepting processes.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:19.680
First, in the electron donating process, fuel molecules will pass the electrons

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.180
to a smaller number of universal electron acceptance,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.500
such as NAD+ or FAD.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.180
The electron accepting process is when you

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:37.750
have a flow of electrons from these few universal electron

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:46.110
acceptors to a final electron acceptor, which is, on this diagram, oxygen.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.900
Let&amp;#39;s take a look at the major parts of the energetic pathways

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.080
in aerobic organisms.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.650
First, we start with glycolysis, that converts glucose into pyruvate.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:06.830
This oxidation of glucose is done by a series of steps, some of which

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:11.490
coupled with the synthesis of ATP.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.010
In these steps, molecules with a high hydrolysis potential

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.530
will transfer their phosphate to a ADP to form ATP.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:25.410
This type of ATP synthesis is called substrate level phosphorylation.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.430
In addition to producing pyruvate and ATP,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.750
the electrons that are produced by the oxidation of glucose

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:40.590
are passed to NAD to form NADH.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:45.520
The second pathway is the citric acid cycle.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.460
During glycolysis, glucose is not fully oxidized.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:54.860
So pyruvate reacts with coenzyme A to former acetyl coenzyme A.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.940
And the acetyl group will feed the citric acid cycle

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.050
to complete the oxidation of glucose.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.740
The major role of the citric acid cycle is

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:14.290
to form a large quantity of these universal electron acceptors, NADH

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.000
and FADH2.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.380
One step in the citric acid cycle is also coupled with the synthesis of GTP

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.050
by substrate level phosphorylation.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.840
Subsequently, GTP will be converted into ATP.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.210
These two pathways are part of the electron donation process.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.560
Now, what about the electron accepting process?

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:46.570
It is the third part of the biochemical pathway that interests us.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:51.000
The electrons stored in NADH and FADH2 are

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.020
passed through a series of protein complex,

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.380
forming the electron transport chain.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.310
This transfer of electrons releases energy

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:07.610
that will lead to the synthesis disease of a ATP by the ATP synthase.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:15.350
In this case, this type of ATP synthesis is called oxidative phosphorylation.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.730
Under some conditions, aerobic organisms,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:27.660
or tissues from aerobic organisms, will operate under anaerobic conditions.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:33.920
So NADH cannot be converted back into NAD,

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.040
because oxidative phosphorylation is not working.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:45.010
The necessary conversion of NADH to NAD+ is made possible by a series

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:50.730
of pathways that are collectively called fermentation.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:57.220
So that is for organisms that have to live in the presence of oxygen--

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.760
aeroboc organisms.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.540
So what is the situation for anaerobic organisms?

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:07.605
They can produce a ATP through glycolysis.

00:05:07.605 --> 00:05:11.640
And, in some cases, through fermentation.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:18.300
But ATP synthesis, powered by the electron transport chain,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.820
is possible, as well.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.080
Anaerobic organisms use diverse mechanisms

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.880
to harvest energy from fuel molecules.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.680
They can transfer electrons from a variety of electron donors

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.490
to a variety of electron acceptors, other than oxygen.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:45.260
For example, denitrifying bacteria use an electron acceptor,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:51.960
nitrate, that is converted into dinitrogen.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.800
This conversion is coupled with the transfer of electrons

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.410
that will power the synthesis of ATP.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:06.770
Don&amp;#39;t think that glucose is the only electron donating fuel molecule.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.620
In fact, cells are remarkably good at harvesting electrons

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.110
from diverse fuel molecules.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:20.280
For example, in aerobic organisms, amino acids and fatty acids

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.070
are converted into acetyl coenzyme A that will feed the citric acid cycle

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:31.520
and lead to the synthesis of a large number of molecules of ATP,

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.900
through oxidative phosphorylation.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:39.500
In conclusion, all the pathways leading to the ATP synthesis

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:44.750
are collectively referred to as cellular respiration.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.200
If the final electron acceptor is oxygen,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.610
this process will be called aerobic respiration.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:57.920
If the electron acceptor is something else then oxygen--

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.430
for example, nitrate, in the case of denitrifying bacteria--

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:07.430
this type of respiration is called anaerobic respiration.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:11.370
The rest of the videos from unit three will

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:16.640
focus on a detailed analysis of the biochemical pathways leading

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.080
to the synthesis of ATP.

